        "SIGNALMENT": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "SIGNALMENT",
          "canonical_heading": "SIGNALMENT",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Affects all breeds of cats and dogs.",
              "pdf_page": 1076,
              "printed_page": 994,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Neonates before the time that the eyelids open (10–14 days postpartum).",
              "pdf_page": 1076,
              "printed_page": 994,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "SIGNS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "SIGNS",
          "canonical_heading": "SIGNS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Upper and lower eyelid margins are fused (physiologic ankyloblepharon) and lids bulge outward because of the accumulation of debris and discharge within the conjunctival sac (Figure 1).",
              "pdf_page": 1076,
              "printed_page": 994,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Mucous to mucopurulent discharge may extrude through a patent opening at the medial canthus.",
              "pdf_page": 1076,
              "printed_page": 994,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Cornea and conjunctiva may be ulcerated.",
              "pdf_page": 1076,
              "printed_page": 994,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "May note adhesions (symblepharon) of the conjunctiva to the cornea or to other areas of the conjunctiva (including that of the nictitans).",
              "pdf_page": 1076,
              "printed_page": 994,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Perforation of the cornea with iris prolapse and collapse of the globe is occasionally seen. Ophthalmia neonatorum. Typical outward-bulging lids due to accumulation of debris and discharge within the conjunctival sac. Source: Ophthalmology Section, Vetsuisse Faculty, University of Zurich.",
              "pdf_page": 1076,
              "printed_page": 994,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
